**Read:** the complete [Calibration of aerosol instruments — Chen, Fletcher and Cheng](../sources/Aerosol-Instrument-Calibration-Chen-Fletcher-Cheng.lingua#r_03PDE8353KQ9HQKJNAHRH99NPP) chapter, followed by [CPC proportionality calibration — Owen, Mulholland and Guthrie](../sources/CPC-Proportionality-Calibration-Owen-Mulholland-Guthrie.lingua#r_7XKKJ1Q7HNDS2BEPSHZDJMTKVE) and [Mobility particle size spectrometers — Wiedensohler and colleagues](../sources/Mobility-Particle-Size-Spectrometers-Wiedensohler.lingua#r_1W06WPADV43B98EF8NSW9RDCWM). Use the chapter as a map of measurement principles, the first paper as a calibration argument and the second as a study of complete measuring systems.

The facility section predicted concentrations and transfers. A measurement now asks whether observations distinguish those predictions from alternatives. Follow material from the region of interest into an inlet, through tubing and conditioning, through the detector, and finally into a reported quantity. Every transformation has a physical meaning.

This section begins with sample volume and particle transport, then develops optical, condensation, aerodynamic and mobility methods. Counting statistics and calibration lead to uncertainty and three investigations. Surface, chemical and microbial methods extend the evidence afterward; a particle count by itself does not answer all those questions.

**Work:** Draw a proposed measurement beside the process it observes. Label what is directly recorded and what is inferred. Keep the raw time, flow and channel information needed to revisit an inference. Identify one change in the process and one change in the measuring system that could produce the same displayed number.

**Return:** [Clean manufacturing and contamination control — from familiar objects to controlled processes](../Clean-manufacturing-and-contamination-control.lingua#r_7D8SQ8BVQDZQBDBGC6DP0JAMZN).
**Continue in clean manufacturing:** [Define the observed population and the sampling boundary](Define-the-observed-population-and-the-sampling-boundary.lingua#r_1YX75Q8RYV60TB8ZPFD0TK10VC).

